{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close in the first ~1–10 days but become increasingly too low beyond ~day 20, especially in rising markets (model underestimates late-horizon upside).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when the market transitions into a strong uptrend (as in the late part of the true paths), the model responds too slowly and keeps projecting a flatter path; apply an upward tilt when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Post-breakout underreaction: after prices break above the recent 30d range, the model tends to cap levels near ~68–70 while outcomes continue to stair-step higher; relax any implicit ceiling by adding a breakout premium.",
    "Mild overestimation in mid-horizon during pullbacks: around local dips (true series falling into mid-60s), the model can be slightly high (expects faster rebound than realized); damp forecasts when recent returns are negative.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are smoother than realized; widen the distribution/allow larger moves, particularly after day ~15 (increase amplitude in the direction of the prevailing trend)."
  ]
}
